## The problem

**SMS and email gave people little say over which alerts reached them, and gave INSO no way to see how its alerts were used**

INSO has always sent alerts by SMS and email, and still does, because in some countries they're the most reliable way to reach people, with patchy or no internet coverage at all. An alert already said what happened, where and what to do. What people couldn't do was shape what reached them, and INSO couldn't see what happened to an alert once it was sent.

**Only whole regions.** Each NGO has one or two people managing its INSO account in the Partners Portal, a web application used on a laptop. They activate regions for teams, so a team in the south only gets alerts from the south. Nothing finer than a region existed: no area around you, no route, no single compound.

**No way to see how alerts were used.** Emails weren't always opened, some never, and SMS has no real read receipts. Country offices learned how their alerts did only by asking: interviewing NGO workers, asking partner NGOs directly and running satisfaction surveys.

**Composing and sending meant a laptop.** INSO staff wrote and sent alerts from a laptop through the same system, which is hard from the field and sometimes impossible. And every SMS cost the country offices money.

## Users

**Three groups, one sending and two receiving**

{% usertable label="Receive – user groups" photos="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/users/01-user-ngo-field-worker.jpg|projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/users/02-user-security-ops-staff.jpg|" alts="An NGO field worker with a backpack walking across a car park|A security officer on a satellite phone next to a field vehicle|" %}
| Who | Needs | Pains before the app |
|---|---|---|
| **NGO field workers** · In the field, on the move, exposed to risk, sometimes with no internet | Stay updated while moving · Focus only on what affects their safety: the road, where they're going, where they sleep | Only whole regions, activated by their NGO's account admin on a laptop · Alerts lost among every other SMS and email |
| **Security and operations staff** · In an office, keeping an eye on teams who travel, advising them by SMS or a call | Monitor specific areas remotely · Track patterns and ongoing situations | Couldn't follow a place they weren't in · No radius or custom area, like the compound where staff live |
| **Shared** · Both groups | Quickly understand what's happening nearby · Trust the accuracy of alerts · Stay updated in real time | High pressure, little time to read · Unclear alerts reduce trust · Phone checks are a security risk |
{% /usertable %}

{% usertable label="Send – user group" photos="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/users/03-user-inso-staff.jpg" alts="An INSO staff member writing an alert on a laptop" %}
| Who | Needs | Pains before the app |
|---|---|---|
| **INSO staff in the country offices** · Collect, validate, compose and send the alerts | Compose and send quickly, from anywhere, with or without data · See how partners use and experience the alerts, maps, filters and saved alerts, without having to ask · Reduce the cost of SMS | Composing and sending meant a laptop and the Partners Portal, hard from the field · No way to tell whether alerts were read or used, only what people said when asked · SMS was costly |
{% /usertable %}

## Where I came in

**A first draft existed, and I came in to bring some structure**

{% ab v="a" %}
When I joined, the engineers had a first draft of the app, only on TestFlight for iOS. It worked, but it hadn't been piloted or tested, and there was no clear scope, requirements, data or timeline. Its features came from conversations with country office colleagues about what could be useful, so they were tied to real needs, but none of it was documented.

What first caught my eye was that it didn't look like INSO, with brown and beige everywhere and no INSO blue. But the more I looked, the more I wanted to put some structure around the problem itself.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
I joined when the engineers had a first draft of the app, only on TestFlight for iOS. It worked, but it hadn't been piloted or tested, and there was no clear scope, requirements, data or timeline. Its features came from conversations with country office colleagues about what could be useful, so they were tied to real needs, but none of it was documented.

What first stood out was that it didn't look like INSO, with brown and beige everywhere and no INSO blue. But the more I looked, the clearer it became that the problem itself needed structure, so that's where I started.
{% /ab %}

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-former-version.jpg" frame="before" alt="The app when I joined: a dense dark list of alerts in capitals, an alert detail packed with text under a small map, and a map that shows a single alert pin." caption="The app when I joined the project team." /%}

{% ab v="a" %}
The engineers had already explored part of the problem: SMS was costly, more and more NGO workers were using smartphones as work phones, and internet coverage was growing in several of INSO's countries. That combination opened up several opportunities that called for an INSO Alerts mobile app. Not another app for the sake of having an app, people don't need that, but the one way to give people a say over what reaches them, to let INSO staff compose and send from anywhere, and to see how alerts are actually used. It's an alternative to SMS and email, not a replacement.

Where I took the discovery further was tracking. I talked with colleagues from the country offices about how they learned whether their alerts were any good, and whether they made NGO workers any safer. It all came from asking: interviews, partner NGOs, satisfaction surveys. Asking matters, in discovery, in testing and after launch, but it's a support layer, not enough on its own. So I pushed for usage tracking from launch, not for the credit, but to understand in detail how people use the alerts, the maps, the filters and saved alerts, without having to ask them.

That structure had limits. I was the only designer, with two other projects on my plate, and I couldn't reach partners, the actual end users, so country office colleagues stood in for them. I couldn't do research the traditional way, so I had to be realistic about where I could help: taking things from the first draft that worked, even without data, and checking the rest through a heuristic evaluation, interviews and Maze tests with country office colleagues, then a survey with partners after launch. {% mark text="I decided to focus on design principles instead" /%} of a full research phase. There was no baseline to compare against either, since SMS and email couldn't be tracked.

We also chose an order. The receiving side came first, NGO field workers and security staff, with the idea that if their feedback was positive enough, management would give us the green light for the sending side. So INSO staff, the senders, waited, and composing came later as its own release.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
The engineers had already explored part of the problem: SMS was costly, more and more NGO workers were using smartphones as work phones, and internet coverage was growing in several of INSO's countries. That combination opened up several opportunities that called for an INSO Alerts mobile app. Not another app for the sake of having an app, people don't need that, but the one way to give people a say over what reaches them, to let INSO staff compose and send from anywhere, and to see how alerts are actually used. An alternative to SMS and email, not a replacement.

Tracking is where I took the discovery further. I sat down with colleagues from the country offices to understand how they learned whether their alerts were any good, and whether they made NGO workers any safer. It all came from asking: interviews, partner NGOs, satisfaction surveys. Asking matters, in discovery, in testing and after launch, but it's a support layer, not enough on its own. So I pushed for usage tracking from launch, not for the credit, but to understand in detail how people use the alerts, the maps, the filters and saved alerts, without having to ask them.

Something else I pushed for was a new way of working: testing the usability of each feature, discussing the findings within the team and with colleagues in the country offices, and agreeing on a clear course of action before any implementation started. That wasn't part of how the team worked before.

That structure had limits: one designer, two other projects on the same plate, and no access to partners, the actual end users, so country office colleagues stood in for them. Traditional research wasn't an option, so I had to be realistic about where I could help: keeping what worked in the first draft, even without data, and checking the rest through a heuristic evaluation, interviews and Maze tests with country office colleagues, then a survey with partners after launch. {% mark text="I decided to focus on design principles instead" /%} of a full research phase. There was no baseline to compare against either, since SMS and email couldn't be tracked.

We also chose an order. The receiving side came first, NGO field workers and security staff, with the idea that if their feedback was positive enough, management would give us the green light for the sending side. So INSO staff, the senders, had to wait, and I designed composing later as its own release.
{% /ab %}

