## Alerts

**The main screen had all the information, but it was hard to read and scan**

{% block title="Eight alerts squeezed on one screen" %}
Eight alerts were squeezed on one screen, with titles of 12 to 16 words in a tiny column and small dates and distances fighting for attention. The engineers' idea was to show as much as possible without scrolling, so the question became: see everything at once, or read each alert easily? My first thought was "where should I be looking?" There was no way to search by place either, and no profile or way to reach terms, FAQs or info about the app, just a settings icon at the top right.
{% /block %}

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-old-alerts-screen.jpg" frame="before" size="text" alt="The old alerts list, annotated: inconsistent typography, low spacing between items, and location, distance and time not prioritised." caption="Alerts screen before redesign, showing density and unclear hierarchy" /%}

{% tablebox label="table" %}
| Alerts list, former design | Redesign approach |
|---|---|
| Five type styles competed in a 70px row: bold all caps titles, small caps locations, all caps dates, tiny right-aligned distance and time | One title style in sentence case, and a clear secondary level for the rest |
| Location, the thing people scan for, was the smallest element, under a four-line title | Location first, then a short title, then time |
| Alerts were separated by a hairline with almost no padding, so rows merged | More space and a clearer boundary between rows |
| No search, no filter and no account area, just an unlabelled orange circle and a settings icon | Search at the top, a simpler filter, and an entry for the profile, terms and help |
| Every alert had the same warning icon, with no cue for new or unseen alerts and no way to save | A marker for unseen alerts, a Recent Alerts block, and a save button on each alert |
{% /tablebox %}

Saved alerts weren't in the first draft. I asked the country office colleagues, who stood in for partners, how people could keep track of the alerts they care about when they all mix together, and we landed on pinning them. It started as one Saved page in the bottom menu, next to Alerts and Settings.

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/01-alerts_first_view.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/04-alerts_search_results.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/06-alerts_filter_off.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/07-alerts_filter_date_picker.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/08-alerts_filtered.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alerts-and-filters/11-map_explore_loaded.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The new alerts list: location first, then a short title, then time.|Search results for Ukraine.|The filter: programmes and regions, and a date range.|The date picker, with quick options like last week.|The list, filtered.|The map with the alerts in the area." caption="The new alerts list: built for scanning, with search at the top, a simpler filter and a list/map toggle." /%}

{% reactions set="inso-alerts-please" /%}

{% block source="10 of 10 filtered by location in 22 s on average, ease 9.3/10 · 13 filtered by date in 19 s, ease 8.2/10 · Maze tests with country office colleagues" %}
Filtering was quick and easy: testers found alerts by place or by date in about 20 seconds.
{% /block %}

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-flow-2-ut-test-01.jpg" related=true alt="Maze results for filtering alerts by location: the task scenario next to the screens testers went through, with a 100% success rate." caption="Task-based usability test scenario and results for filtering alerts by location" /%}

## Alert detail

**I am in an alert. What information will help me the most?**

{% reactions set="inso-alert-detail" /%}

{% tablebox label="table" %}
| Alert detail, former design | Redesign approach |
|---|---|
| The map filled the top third, so the facts came after it | A summary first (what, where, when, how far), with the map moved lower |
| Two pins, teal and red, with no legend | One pin, with the place name and distance below |
| The latest update and advice were buried under five lower-value fields | Full location, incident type, latest update, latest advice, in the order the interviews showed |
| The date and time showed three times in three formats, and one field was empty | One date and time, and the empty field is gone |
| A raw system label (ALERT_UPDATE) and an email address served as the banner | A clear "Alert update" tag, and an "Alerts history" section |
| Body text was justified with uneven margins, and headings looked like body text | Left-aligned text with consistent margins, and clear headings with space around them |
{% /tablebox %}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/01-single-alert.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/02-single-alert.jpg" fulls="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/01-single-alert-full.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/alert-details/02-single-alert-full.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The redesigned alert detail: an Alert tag and the place, the title, date, time and distance on top, then full location, incident type, latest update and latest advice under clear headings, with the map and its one pin lower down.|An alert update: an Alert update tag on top, the same order of facts, and an Alerts history section at the end listing the earlier alerts." captions="A summary first (what, where, when, how far), one date and time, then the latest update and advice in the order the interviews showed, with the map lower and one pin. Enlarge to see the whole screen.|An update gets a clear \"Alert update\" tag instead of a system label, and the earlier alerts sit in an \"Alerts history\" section. Left-aligned text with clear headings throughout." /%}

## Maps

**A map adds the where, for people who need it**

Most people never open the map. By default the app does what SMS and email did: it sends you the alerts from the regions you subscribed to, and that is the main use. On top of that it adds a map layer and more control, like choosing the areas you want alerts from and the map mode that fits what you're doing. The map is for people who need to see where an alert comes from, follow one area, or explore during a mission, and who would otherwise need another map tool. About 1 in 6 people who used the app have opened a map.

The old map had two tools, Proximity and Polygon. To look anywhere else, you had to open a single alert from the list and jump to its location.

### Explore: what's happening along my route?

{% ab v="a" %}
A heuristic evaluation of the old map turned up three things that mattered here. You couldn't explore freely, because the map only opened as a tool for setting a radius or drawing an area. You couldn't select an alert on the map and read it there. And you couldn't move around the map and then switch back to the list to see only the alerts from the place you had moved to.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
My heuristic evaluation of the old map turned up three things that mattered here. You couldn't explore freely, because the map only opened as a tool for setting a radius or drawing an area. You couldn't select an alert on the map and read it there. And you couldn't move around the map and then switch back to the list to see only the alerts from the place you had moved to.
{% /ab %}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/former-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/former-2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/former-3.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/former-4.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/former-5.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/former-6.jpg" landscape=true frame="before" alts="The old alerts list.|The old map, dark, before any alert is chosen.|The old map: Proximity, a radius around your location.|The old map settings.|A note on the old map: it had two tools, Proximity and Polygon, and to look anywhere else you had to open a single alert from the list.|The old map: Polygon." caption="The former map: a tool for setting a radius or drawing an area, not for exploring." /%}

{% ab v="a" %}
Explore fixes all three. You can move around the map freely, tap an alert to read its details on the map and open each one separately, and go to any place and switch back to the list to load the alerts from that area. None of this was possible in the former design, and colleagues in the country offices raised the same things when we tested. Testing also caught two smaller things: scrolling an alert also moved the map, and alerts without a location never showed up on it, so list and map counts didn't match.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
I designed Explore to fix all three. You can move around the map freely, tap an alert to read its details on the map and open each one separately, and go to any place and switch back to the list to load the alerts from that area. None of this was possible in the former design, and colleagues in the country offices raised the same things when we tested. Testing also caught two smaller things: scrolling an alert also moved the map, and alerts without a location never showed up on it, so list and map counts didn't match.
{% /ab %}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/01-alerts_first_view.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/02-alerts_list_instruct_03.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/03-map_explore_loading.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/04-map_explore_loaded.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/05-map_explore_alert-selected.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-explore/06-last-visual-alerts_list_view_explore-enabled.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The alerts list, with a Map button at the bottom.|The guide: explore alerts in a map view.|The map opens in Explore mode, loading.|The map with the alerts in the area.|An alert tapped on the map, its details in a card.|Back on the list, Explore is enabled: the list shows the alerts of the area chosen on the map." caption="Explore: move around the map freely, tap an alert to read it there, and switch back to the list for that area." /%}

{% block source="8 of 8 completed · 33 s on average · ease 8.6/10 · Maze test with country office colleagues" %}
Testers found the map from the alerts list and explored it freely, with no drop-offs.
{% /block %}

### Proximity: I'm not in the area I want to monitor.{% br /%}Can I still stay informed?

{% ab v="a" %}
Proximity shows alerts within a radius of your live location, or of a pin you drop somewhere else. The first version had two ways to set that radius: on the map, and with a slider in the filter menu a bit like dating apps do. Most testers went to the map through the Map button on the alerts list, so the slider went and the radius now lives in one place.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
Proximity shows alerts within a radius of your live location, or of a pin you drop somewhere else. The first version had two ways to set that radius: on the map, and with a slider in the filter menu a bit like dating apps do. Most testers went to the map through the Map button on the alerts list, so I removed the slider and kept the radius in one place.
{% /ab %}

{% block source="Maze test with country office colleagues" %}
6 test participants were able to set a radius around a place they weren't in, in 53 seconds on average, with an ease score of 6.8 out of 10.
{% /block %}

While the participants were thinking aloud during the test, I understood that most of them, if not all, read the little intro and voiced their understanding of the proximity mode. But it's one thing to understand something and another to know how to put it into practice. So I learned that the proximity intro needed to be a proper step-by-step tutorial. Nothing too extensive, to avoid killing engagement, but not a pure intro either, without showing how to use the mode.

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/01-map_explore.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/02-map_prox_view_01.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/03-map_prox_view_intruct_1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/04-map_prox_view_default.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/05-map_prox_view_active.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-01/06-alerts_options_applied_indication.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The map in Explore mode.|Proximity, with Your location and Custom location, and a radius slider.|The single pop-up: Alerts by proximity.|Proximity: a radius around your location.|Proximity: the radius set, with the alerts inside it.|Back on the list: a 50km proximity filter is on." caption="The first version I tested: one pop-up to explain Proximity." /%}

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/01-map_explore.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/02-map_prox_view_01.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/03-map_prox_view_intruct_1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/04-map_prox_view_intruct_2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/05-map_prox_view_intruct_3.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/06-map_prox_view_default.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/07-map_prox_view_active.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-prox/iteration-02/08-alerts_options_applied_indication.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The map in Explore mode.|Proximity, with Your location and Custom location.|Tutorial, 1 of 3: see what's around you.|Tutorial, 2 of 3.|Tutorial, 3 of 3.|Proximity: a radius around your location.|Proximity: the radius set, with the alerts inside it.|Back on the list: a 50km proximity filter is on." caption="The redesign: a three-step tutorial shows how to use live and custom location." /%}

{% reactions set="inso-proximity-maybe" /%}

{% block source="Interviews with 5 country office colleagues who hadn't seen the app before · updated design and prototype" %}
The tutorial cleared up the difference between live and custom location, and colleagues were more satisfied with the feature.
{% /block %}

### Geofence: how do I track one specific area, like a residential campus?

Let's take an organisation that wants to follow what happens around the compound where its staff live. They can draw a shape on the map to cover the exact area, and keep getting alerts from inside it.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/former-geofence.jpg" frame="before" size="text" badges="37,86.3|50,30.5|57.2,43.7|61.5,79|60.9,14.5" spots="38.3,61.8,83.6,89.4|30.4,69.8,21,40|45,65.4,40.5,53.2|63.2,69.2,77.6,89.2|40,60,15.4,19.4" alt="The former Polygon screen: a map with a drawn shape, two unlabelled orange buttons, a grey note saying press the back button to save the polygon filter, and a Proximity / Polygon switch at the top." caption="The former Polygon mode, numbered to match the table below." /%}

**Polygon mode (Geofence)**

| Geofence (Polygon) former design | Redesign approach (see slideshow below) |
|---|---|
| {% n v="1" /%}Saving meant pressing the Back button ("Press the back button to save the polygon filter"). It was hidden and backwards, since Back normally cancels. | An explicit Confirm button, and a "Geofence established" message afterwards |
| {% n v="2" /%}No instructions. The first screen was just a map, with no hint to tap to drop pins, or that zooming first matters. | A three-step tutorial: zoom, drop pins, confirm |
| {% n v="3" /%}No result feedback. You drew a shape but couldn't tell how many alerts it contained. | "76 alerts in the selected area", with the alert markers inside the shape |
| {% n v="4" /%}Two unlabelled orange buttons (an undo arrow and a "+"). Their purpose wasn't clear, and orange didn't match the rest of the app. | An undo button, plus labelled "Clear all" and "Edit geofence" buttons |
| {% n v="5" /%}The name "Polygon" is a geometry term, not a task. | Renamed Geofence, one of three clear tabs: Explore, Proximity, Geofence |

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/01-map_view_geofence_1_blank.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/02-map_view_geofence_instruct.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/03-map_view_geofence_instruct_map_zoomed.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/04-map_view_geofence_instruct_first_pin.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/05-map_view_geofence_instruct_first_line.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/06-map_view_geofence_instruct_geofence.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/07-map_view_geofence_instruct_geofence-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/08-map_view_geofence_1_step_2-2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/09-map_view_geofence_1_step_2.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/10-map_view_geofence_1_step_2-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/simple/map-geo/11-geofence_based_alerts.jpg" landscape=true frame="after" alts="The map in Geofence mode, before drawing.|Tutorial, 1 of 3: select your zoom level.|Tutorial, 1 of 3, the map zoomed in.|Tutorial, 2 of 3: press to drop pins, the first pin dropped.|Tutorial, 2 of 3: a second pin, joined by a line.|Tutorial, 3 of 3: confirm your selection, with a Confirm button.|Tutorial, 3 of 3: the area with its alerts, Clear all and Edit geofence.|Drawing: a pin dropped, 76 alerts in the selected area.|The area drawn, with a Confirm button.|The area confirmed, with its alerts, Clear all and Edit geofence.|Back on the list: Geofence established." caption="The redesigned Geofence: a three-step tutorial, a Confirm button, the number of alerts in the area, and clear labels." /%}

{% ab v="a" %}
It was also the hardest to learn: 5 of 8 testers finished the task but none of them took the intended path. Two asked for a video, which is heavy on weak connections, so Geofence got the same kind of three-step animated tutorial instead.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
It was also the hardest to learn: 5 of 8 testers finished the task but none of them took the intended path. Two asked for a video, which is heavy on weak connections, so I gave Geofence the same kind of three-step animated tutorial instead.
{% /ab %}

{% block source="From the same 5 interviews with country office colleagues" %}
Colleagues understood Geofence, but saw it as relevant only to those who work actively with maps.
{% /block %}

### What people actually use

The usage tracking shows that, across releases, Explore takes most of the map traffic, with Proximity and Geofence well behind. That matches what partners ranked as most important in the post-launch survey, where Geofence came last (9 of 30), as expected for a tool for a handful of specialists.

## After the first release

{% ab v="a" %}
The project didn't stop here, since continuous feedback from country office colleagues kept shaping the app after launch:
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
The project didn't stop here. I kept shaping the app after launch with continuous feedback from country office colleagues:
{% /ab %}

- Notifications limited to your regions or to a saved map search, after a short survey with country office staff. People do open them: over 100,000 times from notifications in three years.
- Unseen alerts pointed out, a new Recent Alerts block, and a restyled date picker with quick options (last 3 days, last week, last month) that shows clearly when a filter is on.
- Animated tutorials in every map mode.

{% sequence images="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/new-alerts-1.jpg, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/new-alerts-2.png, projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/sequences/new-alerts-3.jpg" alts="The alerts list while browsing.|A New alerts button appears at the top of the list.|The list with a Recent Alerts block on top, holding the alerts not seen yet." caption="New alerts pointed out live while you browse the list, with the Recent Alerts block separating what's new from what you've read." /%}

### What about creating and sending alerts from the app?

This was part of the problem from the start, but we agreed to focus on the NGO side first. With management sign-off I designed the first version in early 2025, and it was released in September 2025 together with login through an INSO account: a simple form with a map pin and distribution lists, plus updates to existing alerts.

{% figure src="projects/mitigating-risk-with-location-based-alerts/inso-app-compose.jpg" alt="Composing an alert: the alerts list with an INSO ALERT label and a megaphone button, the empty form, the filled form with location and distribution lists, and the sent confirmation." caption="Composing an INSO ALERT from the phone, from the megaphone on the alerts list to the confirmation. Alert text, place names, the map and list sizes are blurred." /%}

## Outcome

### How the app is used today

{% ab v="a" %}
{% block icon="bricks" %}
This is the kind of data that didn't exist with SMS and email. After the launch peak of about 1,600 users, a steady base settled in: around 800 a month through 2024, and 450 to 630 through 2026. About 8 in 10 of them are returning users, so most people who use it keep coming back.
{% /block %}
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
{% block icon="bricks" %}
This is the kind of data that didn't exist with SMS and email, and the reason I pushed for tracking. After the launch peak of about 1,600 users, a steady base settled in: around 800 a month through 2024, and 450 to 630 through 2026. About 8 in 10 of them are returning users, so most people who use it keep coming back.
{% /block %}
{% /ab %}

{% usagechart caption="Monthly active and returning users since launch, rounded. Test and visitor traffic left out." /%}

{% cta href="https://app.ngosafety.org/" label="Take a look" /%}

**What I can't say.** Nothing could be measured before the app, since SMS and email can't be tracked, so I can't say whether it made things better, only that it's used and that people keep coming back. The partner survey below is the asking side, the support layer.

{% block result=true %}
In a post-launch survey of 31 partners, the 29 who rated ease of use gave it an average score of 4.1/5.
{% /block %}

{% block result=true %}
The weakest score was whether the app helped them reach their goal: 21 of 31 gave it a 4 or 5 (average 3.7), and a few said they hadn't really used it yet.
{% /block %}

## Takeaways

{% ab v="a" %}
- **Design for the worst connection first.** Weak networks, stress and no time to learn shaped most decisions, from animated tutorials instead of video to a list you can scan in seconds.
- **Test with proxies, then check with the real people.** Country office colleagues before launch, partners after.
- **Security and usability pull against each other.** Blocking copy and screenshots keeps alerts safe, but partners named it their biggest frustration.
- **Put things in order, on purpose.** The receiving side came first to win the green light for the sending side, but it meant the senders had to wait.
- **What I'd do differently:** run a proper retest of Geofence, and set up tracking before a feature ships.
{% /ab %}

{% ab v="b" %}
- **Design for the worst connection first.** Weak networks, stress and no time to learn shaped most decisions, from animated tutorials instead of video to a list you can scan in seconds.
- **Test with proxies, then check with the real people.** Country office colleagues before launch, partners after.
- **Security and usability pull against each other.** Blocking copy and screenshots keeps alerts safe, but partners named it their biggest frustration.
- **Put things in order, on purpose.** Putting the receiving side first won us the green light for the sending side, but it meant the senders had to wait.
- **What I'd do differently:** run a proper retest of Geofence, and set up tracking before a feature ships.
{% /ab %}
